# Why Facebook Sharer Popups Ignore Your Custom Metadata

When building social sharing functionality, you often encounter a specific friction point: the discrepancy between the metadata your application sends and what the target platform actually displays. In the scenario where an `onClick` handler triggers a popup to `http://www.facebook.com/sharer.php`, it is common for developers to assume that passing parameters via URL query strings (like `p[title]` or `p[url]`) will override the page's native metadata.

However, Facebook's scraper logic prioritizes the content of the linked resource over the parameters passed in the sharer URL. Even if your PHP script successfully redirects to a new URL or sets headers that suggest a different title or image, Facebook's crawler will likely ignore these signals when generating the preview card. Instead, it scrapes the destination page directly, pulling the `<title>` tag and Open Graph tags from the actual HTML of the linked URL. This behavior is by design; platforms want to ensure that shared content accurately reflects the live version of a page rather than a temporary state injected via script parameters.

The code snippet provided demonstrates a common pattern used before Facebook's official SDK was widely adopted or when developers attempted to bypass strict Open Graph requirements manually:

```html
<script type="text/javascript">
function fbs_click(width, height) {
    var leftPosition, topPosition;
    leftPosition = (window.screen.width / 2) - ((width / 2) + 10);
    topPosition = (window.screen.height / 2) - ((height / 2) + 50);

    // Calculate position to center the popup
    var windowFeatures = "status=no,height=" + height + ",width=" + width + 
                          ",resizable=no,left=" + leftPosition + ",top=" + topPosition + 
                          ",screenX=" + leftPosition + ",screenY=" + topPosition + 
                          ",toolbar=no,menubar=no,scrollbars=no,location=no,directories=no";

    // The critical issue lies here: passing u and t directly
    var u = location.href;
    var t = document.title;

    window.open('http://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u='+encodeURIComponent(u)+'&t='+encodeURIComponent(t),'sharer', windowFeatures);
    return false;
}
</script>
```

In this implementation, `location.href` captures the current URL, and `document.title` grabs the browser's title. While this works for generic sharing, it fails when you need to inject custom data like "Google" as the title while keeping the actual page content different. The JavaScript simply passes whatever is currently in the DOM to Facebook. If the user is on a page where the title has been dynamically changed by PHP or JS, but the underlying HTML hasn't updated before the click event fires, you might get stale data.

Furthermore, relying on `p[title]` and `p[url]` query parameters is an outdated approach for modern web standards. Facebook deprecated support for these specific URL parameters in favor of the Open Graph protocol. The platform now expects the linked page to contain valid `<meta property="og:title">`, `<meta property="og:url">`, and `<meta property="og:image">` tags. If these are missing or if the platform decides to ignore them (often due to canonicalization issues or strict referrer policies), it falls back to scraping the raw HTML.

To ensure the popup displays the correct data, you must treat the sharer link as a request to validate and display the destination page's metadata, not as a vehicle for injecting arbitrary strings. If you require dynamic content that doesn't match the current page's `<title>` tag, the solution is not to pass it via the sharer URL parameters. Instead, you should ensure the destination URL itself returns the correct Open Graph data.

For Laravel applications or any modern stack where routing and views are decoupled, this often means ensuring your controller sets the proper response headers or that your view renders the correct `<head>` block before the user clicks share. If you absolutely must customize the share intent without changing the destination URL, you might consider using a dedicated sharing service API rather than hitting Facebook's sharer endpoint directly with custom parameters, as this allows for more granular control over how the preview is constructed client-side before opening the window.

Ultimately, attempting to force data into the `p[...]` parameters against Facebook's scraper logic will always result in the platform displaying its own scraped metadata. The robust path forward is aligning your page's Open Graph tags with the content you intend to share.
